The CVE-2021-44228 vulnerability in Apache Log4j version 2.14.1 arises due to the use of the `JndiLookup` class, which can be exploited to perform a deserialization attack. When an attacker sets the `log4j.configurationFile` property to a malicious `JndiLookup` class, it can lead to remote code execution. This can be achieved using the following Java code:

```java
import org.apache.logging.log4j.core.LoggerContext;
import org.apache.logging.log4j.core.config.Configurator;

public class Log4jCVE {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Configurator.setAllLog4jLoggers(Level.ALL);
        LoggerContext ctx = (LoggerContext) LogManager.getContext(false);
        ctx.setConfigLocation(null);
    }
}
```

Mitigation strategies include upgrading to Apache Log4j version 2.15.0 or later and modifying the `log4j2.xml` configuration file to remove the `JndiLookup` class.

Real-world example: This vulnerability was used in the wild to perform a large-scale Log4Shell attack in 2021.
